controversy-and-consensus-lines-2 = cons "Avik Jain" (cons "" (cons (quoted (cons "Reading *controversy* solely as adversarial combat (" (cons "turned against" (cons " in the courtroom fight) flattens its classical meaning: dialogue — turns held with polarity." nil)))) (cons "" (cons "Roman rhetorical practice demonstrates this directly. A *controversia* was a foundational exercise in inquiry: taking a case and arguing it rigorously from both sides in turn, as collected in Seneca the Elder's *Controversiae*. Neither pole lost; understanding emerged precisely from turning the same question through both poles." (cons "" (cons "Etymologically, *controversia* is Latin (*contrā* + *versus*, from *vertere*, to turn); its Greek counterparts are dialectic (*dialegesthai*, to reason through) and *antilogia*, setting argument beside counter-argument so a question reveals its full geometry between speakers." (cons "" (cons "Understood at their roots, **controversy and consensus** are not opposites; they are two phases of a single rotation:" (cons "- **Controversy** is the turning: positions examined from opposite poles in alternating verses (*versus*)." (cons "- **Consensus** is *con* + *sentire*, perceiving and feeling together — the exact Latin counterpart of Greek *syn-aisthēsis*. Consensus is interpersonal synesthesia: two observers perceiving one structure together." (cons "" (cons "A controversy carried all the way through arrives naturally at consensus: the alternating turns, perceived together, reveal themselves as faces of one rotation — *universe* (*unus* + *versus*, turned into one)." (cons "" (cons "Controversy therefore implies no incompatibility. Positions turned from opposite poles are compatible in the strict etymological sense (*com-pati*, receiving and bearing the other's standpoint). Conflict is merely controversy arrested halfway, when two poles refuse to keep turning." (cons "" (cons "Three consequences connect this directly to hyperactivity:" (cons "- **Expository method.** Stating an established truth at the exact pole from which unexamined social reflexes flinch forces inquiry to turn through the suppressed face rather than stopping at a half-turn." (cons "- **The interaction net.** An active pair is two agents meeting face-to-face at their principal ports; each reduction step is a polarized turn between two agents, making computation itself a dialogue of turns." nil))))))))))))))))))
